Another potentially good game bites the dust.

Playing a Priest on a UNIX NH3.0hPL6, I went into a used armor shop
with my kitten.  My inventory was: some armor (being worn), a pick-axe
(weapon in hand), and a leash (in use).  Inside the shop, I dug down
twice with my pick-axe.  The shopkeeper came and grabbed my backpack,
but my kitten still went down with me.  On the next level, I checked my
inventory to discover that the leash was missing.  However, my kitten
continued to behave as though it was on leash.

I tried to get rid of the kitten, but nothing worked.  Well, I climbed
up and went into the shop again.  While I was busy collecting all the
goodies, my kitten ran over the trapdoor I dug before.  Sure enough, we
both reached the "impossible" piece of code in trap.c, and ended up
with a Segmentation Fault:

> 599   leport_pet(mtmp)
> 600   gister struct monst *mtmp;
> 601   {
> 602   	register struct obj *otmp;
> 603
> 604   	if(mtmp->mleashed) {
> 605   	    otmp = get_mleash(mtmp);
> 606   	    if(!otmp)
> 607   		impossible("%s is leashed, without a leash.", Monnam(mtmp));
                       ^^^^^^^^^^ maybe not??

The bugs in NetHack are becoming more and more obscure...

